course: dictionary
lesson: 13655
title: Inflected words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An inflected word changes its form to show grammar, such as when something happened or how many there are. «Walked» is an inflected form of «walk», because the ending -ed shows the action happened in the past.
explain: The base word is the plain form you would find at the top of a dictionary entry. «Cat» becomes «cats» when there is more than one. «Big» becomes «bigger» and «biggest» when we compare things. Each new form is inflected.
explain: Some languages change their words far more than English does. In Latin, German and Ukrainian, word endings change a lot to show the job each word does in a sentence. English changes fewer words, so spotting the patterns is useful.
explain: To check, ask whether the ending shows something new about grammar. «The sun is hot» uses the base form. «The sun is hotter today» uses an inflected form, because -er compares two things.
fact: Inflected means changed in form to show grammar, such as tense, number or person.
fact: «Walked» is an inflected form of «walk», showing the past.
fact: Cat and cats, run and ran and running, big and bigger and biggest: each group is one word in different inflected forms.
fact: A highly inflected language, like Latin, German or Ukrainian, changes its word endings a lot.
fact: In speech, an inflected voice changes pitch, going up at the end of a question.
exercise: «The cats sleep all day.» Which word is an inflected form of cat? | cat | *cats | day | sleep
exercise: Which form of big compares three or more things? | large | bigger | big | *biggest
exercise: «She walked to school.» Which word shows the walking happened in the past? | school | She | to | *walked
test: Which word is an inflected form of play, showing it happened yesterday? | play | *played | player | playground
test: Which word is the base form, the one you would find in a dictionary? | cooked | cooks | cooking | *cook
exam: Which sentence has an inflected word showing more than one? | The mat is red. | *Two cats sit on the mat. | Sit down now. | I see a cat.
